#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092023} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186607568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186613762}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092024} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186605114}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093402 :tutorial/content "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\t=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\nA^{-1} =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39b17ee8-086a-5282-a414-c88d455b6107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093404 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y &=3 \\\\ 6x+3y &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nCan we use the matrix method of $\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ to find a solution to this system?\n\nThis system can be represented in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo use this matrix method, we need to find $A^{-1}.$ However\n\n$$\n\\det (A) = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 6 =0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is not invertible. So we cannot use the matrix method to find a solution to the system.\n\nIn general, if the coefficient matrix $A$ is not invertible, there is no unique solution. This means that either\n\n- a solution may not exist, or\n\n- there may be infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5e2bff7-df5b-5628-92e3-a50ca8dd6be8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3066 :tutorial/title "Systems of Equations Where the Coefficient Matrix is Singular"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115234 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115065} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299715} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186642348}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115235 :kno